@*
    Every face a species class can wear.

    A row of pictures rather than chips, because what a reader came to a species page for is what the
    thing looks like - and a name beside each of thirty faces is thirty names to read past. The name
    is in the panel, where it is there when it is wanted.

    Fixed-size tiles in an auto-filling grid, which is how every other grid of faces in the app is
    laid out: a portrait picker draws a hundred of these and they do not stretch.

    Each tile is a square crop of a picture that is not square, so at this size a face is often most
    of what is missing from it. Pointing at one shows the whole thing, through the same popover the
    chips use: the browser puts it in the top layer, so it is not cut off by the table it is inside.
*@

<div class="sem-wiki-gallery @Shape">
    @for (var index = 0; index < Faces.Count; index++)
    {
        var face = Faces[index];
        var id = $"{_scope}-{index}";
        var url = Session.Data.AssetUrl(face.Icon!);

        @*
            A button rather than the picture alone. A pointer is not the only way to reach this:
            popovertarget gives a tap its panel, which is the whole of how a phone reads one, and a
            button is the thing a keyboard can arrive at.

            The press is stopped, because the row underneath picks the entry out and pointing at a
            face is not choosing anything.
        *@
        <span class="sem-wiki-face">
            <button type="button" popovertarget="@id" aria-describedby="@id" title="@face.Name"
                    @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                <img src="@url" alt="@face.Name" loading="lazy">
            </button>

            <div id="@id" popover="auto" role="tooltip" class="sem-portrait-detail">
                <img src="@url" alt="" loading="lazy">
                <span>@face.Name</span>
            </div>
        </span>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The faces, each of which is known to have a picture.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required IReadOnlyList<EmpireChoice> Faces { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What shape the pictures are, which decides the tile.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A face is taller than it is wide and a square tile crops it about as well as anything can. A
    /// ship is the other way round, and the same square took a fifth off each end - the nose and the
    /// engines, which are most of what tells one set's ships from another's.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public string Shape { get; set; } = "scene";

    /// <summary>
    /// What this gallery's panels are named after, so that no two on a page collide.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Not the face's own key. The species page draws three hundred and sixty-nine of these across
    /// forty-two galleries and a portrait may be worn by more than one class - two panels under one
    /// id, and <c>getElementById</c> hands both anchors the first of them.
    /// </remarks>
    private readonly string _scope = $"face-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";
}
